── Thinking ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Nine more REPL-only commands are now refused for free when typed at the shell with one of their own subcommands. Before this, yoyo checkpoint save or yoyo git status started a paid model turn with write tools attached. Build, the full cargo test run, clippy with -D warnings and fmt --check all pass.

Which verbs I checked. I took every REPL command, removed the ones that already work as shell subcommands and the seven that were already guarded, and got 48, not the 50 #936 quotes. #936 probably counted a different snapshot of the command list; I didn't pin down the exact difference.

| Result | Count | Verbs |
|---|---|---|
| Gated, each on its own existing *_SUBCOMMANDS list | 9 | checkpoint, fork, bg, revisit, spawn, history, stash, pr, git |
| Left alone because their subcommand words start normal prompts | 4 | plan ("plan on migrating…"), refactor ("refactor extract the parser"), copy ("copy code from a.rs…"), web ("web search for…") |
| Still billed: they have no subcommand list to gate on | 35 | add, mark, mcp, profile, and the rest, all named in the commit message |

Five verbs the task listed as candidates (goal, watch, skill, lint, config) already work as shell commands, so I didn't touch them.

One change beyond the table: a flag in the second position (such as --bg) never triggers the refusal. spawn's list includes flags like --bg and -o for tab-completion, and a command-line flag shouldn't be read as a subcommand.

Tests (in src/dispatch_near_miss.rs):
• One test covers all nine new verbs. It first checks each has at least one non-flag subcommand, then checks every subcommand word is refused and that the message names the yoyo -p "..." workaround.
• A second test checks that prose reaches the model unchanged, with exact-match assertions: one sentence per new verb, one per excluded verb, and one spawn --bg … case.
• I emptied checkpoint's list as a deliberate break, and the new test plus the existing whole-table check both failed. The file was restored in the same command, with no leftover marker.

The CHANGELOG entry is under Unreleased.

Something odd you should know about: twice, my edit adding the new table entries disappeared from the file after the auto-check ran, and one guard line ended up in the file twice. I re-applied the edits with a script, removed the duplicate, and committed right away. The final commit has the full table and a single copy of the guard. I don't know what reverted the edits; the auto-check tool is the likely suspect.

The history has four WIP commits before the final one (9e79982c). I added those after the edits kept disappearing so the work would survive; you may want to squash them. #936 should stay open for the 35 verbs with no subcommand list.
